      "text": "Given the two points on the line at \\( t = 2 \\) and \\( t = 3 \\) as \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to determine the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\).\n\nFirst, let's express the parameter \\( t \\) in terms of the coordinates. The vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x(t) \\\\ y(t) \\end{pmatrix}\\) on the line can be written as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x(t) \\\\ y(t) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} x(2) + (t-2)x_1 \\\\ y(2) + (t-2)y_1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is the direction vector of the line.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x(2) \\\\ y(2) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\begin{pmatrix} x(3) \\\\ y(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) can be computed by:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\\\ y_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{1} \\begin{pmatrix} x(3) - x(2) \\\\ y(3) - y(2) \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{1} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the general form of the vector on the line is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x(t) \\\\ y(t) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2(t-2) \\\\ 4 - 8(t-2) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 2t - 4 \\\\ 4 - 8t + 16 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 + 2t \\\\ 20 - 8t \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\):\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x(-7) \\\\ y(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 + 2(-7) \\\\ 20 - 8(-7) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 - 14 \\\\ 20 + 56 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
