      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We are given the parameterized line and asked to find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\). The line is parameterized such that the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\), and the vector at \\( t = 3 \\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTo find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\), we need to determine the direction vector of the line. The direction vector can be found by subtracting the vector at \\( t = 2 \\) from the vector at \\( t = 3 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - 1 \\\\ -4 - 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\\) represents the change in position per unit change in \\( t \\). To find the change in position from \\( t = 2 \\) to \\( t = -7 \\), we note that as \\( t \\) changes from 2 to -7, it decreases by 9 units (since \\( -7 - 2 = -9 \\)). The change in the direction vector is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Change in direction vector} = 9 \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 \\cdot 2 \\\\ 9 \\cdot (-8) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 18 \\\\ -72 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add this change in direction vector to the vector at \\( t = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 18 \\\\ -72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 18 \\\\ 4 - 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 \\\\ -68 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 19 \\\\ -68 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
