      "text": "This problem can be solved using the concept of parametric equations of a line. The vector on the line at \\( t \\) is given by \\(\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\mathbf{v}(2) + (t-2) \\mathbf{d}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is the direction vector of the line.\n\nHere's the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Find the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\)**:\n   - At \\( t = 2 \\), the vector is \\(\\mathbf{v}(2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n   - At \\( t = 3 \\), the vector is \\(\\mathbf{v}(3) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n   - The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) can be found by subtracting \\(\\mathbf{v}(2)\\) from \\(\\mathbf{v}(3)\\):\n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{d} = \\mathbf{v}(3) - \\mathbf{v}(2) = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} - \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}.\n     \\]\n\n2. **Write the parametric equation of the line**:\n   - The line can be parameterized as:\n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{v}(t) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (t-2) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}.\n     \\]\n   - To find the vector at \\( t = -7 \\), substitute \\( t = -7 \\) into the parametric equation:\n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{v}(-7) = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-7-2) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + (-9) \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} -18 \\\\ 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 - 18 \\\\ 4 + 72 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}.\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the vector on the line at \\( t = -7 \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -17 \\\\ 76 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
